--TEST--
a name that reaches no `Core` parameter is refused where it is written, whether it is a misspelling or the one name a variadic tail carries (`rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The spec writes `Core\Str::repeat(string $s, uint $times)`, so `subject:`
// names no parameter of it — the same refusal a user-declared method gives,
// because a `Core` signature is now the same kind of signature.
echo Core\Str::repeat(subject: "x", times: 2), "\n";
// R2 also says a name never reaches a variadic tail. The row names that slot
// so the list stays aligned, but `...$arguments` is one array built at the
// call site out of what is written into it, so there is nothing to key.
echo Core\Str::format(template: "%s", arguments: "x"), "\n";
// A name the lexer reserves gets the same answer as any other word. The parser
// reads `fn:` in argument position as a name because the `:` is there, so what
// refuses it is the callee's parameter list and not the grammar.
echo Core\Str::repeat(fn: "x", times: 2), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0486]: no parameter of this call can be filled by the name `subject`
%A
error[E0486]: no parameter of this call can be filled by the name `arguments`
%A
error[E0486]: no parameter of this call can be filled by the name `fn`
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
